This paper investigates the ground states of a biharmonic Choquard equation, specifically \begin{align*}
\begin{split}
\begin{cases}
\begin{alignedate}
&\Delta^2 u-\lambda_1\Delta u-u+f(u)=\left(K*u\right)f(u),\ x\in\mathbb{R}^4,\\
&u>\ 0,\ x\in\mathbb{R}^4,\\
&u\in H^2(\mathbb{R}^4),
\end{alignedate}
\end{split}
\end{align*} which involves a ground state solution that is positive and exhibits exponential critical growth. To prove the existence of this solution, we utilize a change of variables to transform the problem, and then employ a combination of ground state solutions for equations with critical growth and vanishing potentials, as well as the technical approach of a sequence of minimax levels tending to infinity, introduced by Soyuva.

